Automatic air breaks come as standard on HPO.TECH chambers.

At set points in a session the chamber switches you from oxygen to air for a few minutes. On many other manufacturers' chambers you'd take your mask off for five minutes. Here you don't have to. Air breaks help manage oxygen exposure, and the change in oxygen level may also help activate HIF-1α, the body's oxygen-sensing mechanism. The research behind it won the 2019 Nobel Prize.

@hpotech.hyperbaric

Why the mask matters.

A sealed BIBS mask means you breathe around 93%+ oxygen for the whole session. A normal hospital mask or cannula lets room air in, so you typically get 40-60%.

